{"id": "077e9b5e5b803672:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:6fdc0a34ad75c037", "source_id": "077e9b5e5b803672", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/retirement-toolkit/employer-sponsored-plans/traditional-and-roth-401k-plans", "topic": "401(k) Plans", "subtopic": "401(k) Plans", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "With a traditional 401(k), the tax benefit is upfront. With a Roth 401(k), the tax benefit comes later.", "question": null, "answer": "A 401(k) is an employer-sponsored retirement plan that lets employees choose among investment options. Many plans offer both traditional and Roth accounts, which differ in when their tax benefits apply. Traditional 401(k) contributions and investment earnings are tax-deferred, so taxes are paid when savings are withdrawn. Roth 401(k) contributions are made with after-tax dollars; account earnings and withdrawals are generally tax-free.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1179, "output_tokens": 193}}
{"id": "077e9b5e5b803672:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:6fdc0a34ad75c037", "source_id": "077e9b5e5b803672", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/retirement-toolkit/employer-sponsored-plans/traditional-and-roth-401k-plans", "topic": "401(k) Plans", "subtopic": "401(k) Plans", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "In a Roth 401(k) account, employee contributions are not tax-deferred but are made with after-tax dollars. Income earned on the account, from interest, dividends, or capital gains, and withdrawals are generally tax-free.", "question": "How does the tax treatment of a Roth 401(k) differ from a traditional 401(k)?", "answer": "Roth 401(k) contributions are made with after-tax dollars, while traditional 401(k) contributions and investment earnings are tax-deferred and taxed when withdrawn. Income earned in a Roth 401(k) and withdrawals are generally tax-free.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1179, "output_tokens": 227}}
{"id": "077e9b5e5b803672:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:6fdc0a34ad75c037", "source_id": "077e9b5e5b803672", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/retirement-toolkit/employer-sponsored-plans/traditional-and-roth-401k-plans", "topic": "401(k) Plans", "subtopic": "401(k) Plans", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "In a Roth 401(k) account, employee contributions are not tax-deferred but are made with after-tax dollars.", "question": "Misconception: Roth 401(k) contributions are tax-deferred in the same way as traditional 401(k) contributions.", "answer": "Correction: Roth 401(k) contributions are made with after-tax dollars, so they are not tax-deferred. Traditional 401(k) contributions are tax-deferred, with taxes paid when the savings are withdrawn.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1179, "output_tokens": 220}}
{"id": "077e9b5e5b803672:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:7ef4109a52811dbd", "source_id": "077e9b5e5b803672", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/retirement-toolkit/employer-sponsored-plans/traditional-and-roth-401k-plans", "topic": "401(k) Plans", "subtopic": "Traditional vs. Roth 401(k) Accounts", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "In a traditional 401(k) account, employee contributions to a 401(k) plan and any earnings from the investments are tax-deferred. You pay the taxes on contributions and earnings when the savings are withdrawn.", "question": null, "answer": "Traditional and Roth 401(k) accounts differ mainly in when taxes apply. In a traditional account, contributions and investment earnings are tax-deferred, and taxes are paid when savings are withdrawn. In a Roth account, contributions are made with after-tax dollars; income earned in the account and withdrawals are generally tax-free. A plan may let you put money in either option or divide it between both.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1183, "output_tokens": 318}}
