{"id": "077e9b5e5b803672:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:110cac1c5b5d06c4", "source_id": "077e9b5e5b803672", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/retirement-toolkit/employer-sponsored-plans/traditional-and-roth-401k-plans", "topic": "401(k) Plans", "subtopic": "Tax-Deferred Retirement Savings", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "In a traditional 401(k) account, employee contributions to a 401(k) plan and any earnings from the investments are tax-deferred. You pay the taxes on contributions and earnings when the savings are withdrawn.", "question": null, "answer": "In a traditional 401(k), contributions and investment earnings are tax-deferred: taxes on them are postponed until you withdraw the savings. This is the plan’s upfront tax benefit. By contrast, Roth 401(k) contributions are made with after-tax dollars, so they are not tax-deferred.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1179, "output_tokens": 202}}
